# Omastx workflow

- `SPEC.md` is the single source of truth. Follow it exactly; where silent, prefer the
  simplest implementation respecting the architecture, design system, and acceptance criteria.
- At session start, read `docs/plan/PROGRESS.md`, the active milestone file
  (`docs/plan/M*-*.md`), and `docs/plan/DECISIONS.md` before writing code.
- Work milestones strictly in order (M1 → M6). Within a milestone, take the first
  unchecked task unless marked parallelizable.
- Check off milestone tasks only when verified (built/tested), not merely written.
- Before ending a session: update `docs/plan/PROGRESS.md` (current state, milestone table,
  session log row) and check off completed items in the milestone file.
- Record any library pin, schema change, or deviation from SPEC as a new entry in
  `docs/plan/DECISIONS.md`. Never rewrite accepted decisions; supersede them.
- Hard rules from SPEC §8: no UI kit / Tailwind / prebuilt chart themes; never shell out
  to `helm` or `kubectl`; never request Kubernetes write permissions; keep the §2.2
  `ArtifactProvider` / `VersionResolver` interfaces exactly as specified.
- Delivery targets: local via `deploy/docker-compose.yml`; Kubernetes via the Helm chart
  in `deploy/chart/omastx`; images published to ghcr.io by GitHub Actions.

## Post-implementation gate (non-negotiable)

After EVERY implementation (feature, fix, refactor — no exceptions), before considering
the work done, run this three-part review and act on the findings:

1. **Tests**: check whether the change warrants new or updated tests (unit, integration,
   table-driven fixtures). Write them. Every API mutation needs an integration test
   against dockerized Postgres (SPEC §7); drift/tag-selection logic must stay ≥ 90%
   coverage. If no test is warranted, state why explicitly.
2. **Security**: review what was just built from a security point of view — secrets
   handling (never logged, never returned, encrypted at rest), authn/authz on new
   endpoints, CSRF on mutations, input validation at boundaries, SQL injection surface,
   container/chart hardening (nonroot, read-only FS, no privilege escalation), and no
   new Kubernetes permissions beyond read-only.
3. **Best practices**: check the change against development conventions (this rules
   folder + SPEC §7), infrastructure practices (Dockerfiles, compose, Helm chart values,
   probes, resource limits), and every integration touched (CI workflows, GHCR delivery,
   registries, Postgres migrations — forward-only, never edit applied ones).

Record the outcome of this gate (findings fixed, or "clean") in the session's wrap-up
notes in `docs/plan/PROGRESS.md`.
